<h2>The proceedings below</h2>

<p>The Family Court at Kanpur Nagar, by order dated 19 March 2025 in Case No. 172 of 2021, directed the husband to pay monthly maintenance of Rs 20,000 to his wife and Rs 10,000 to the couple's minor son. The husband filed a criminal revision. His central defence was Section 125(4): the wife, he alleged, was living in adultery with a named individual, and he relied on photographs and electronic material to prove it.</p>

<h2>Four questions, four answers</h2>

<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Electronic material</h3><p>Received under Section 14 of the Family Courts Act, but never proved — no Section 65-B certificate, no authentication. Receipt is not proof.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The named third party</h3><p>No adverse finding of adultery can be recorded against him unheard. The husband never sought notice to him, so the plea failed at the threshold.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Fresh evidence in revision</h3><p>Declined. Revision tests the order on the existing record; it is not a second trial.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>The quantum</h3><p>No illegality or perversity shown. Rs 20,000 for the wife and Rs 10,000 for the child affirmed.</p></div>
</div>

<p>The judgment's most consequential passage looks beyond the parties. Noting that Section 125 CrPC contains no procedure prescribing how an alleged co-adulterer is to be brought before the court, the Court directed the Registrar General to place the matter before the Chief Justice to consider either framing rules for impleadment or notice, or extending the principle of Rule 6(a) of the Hindu Marriage and Divorce Rules, 1956 — which requires a co-adulterer to be joined in a divorce petition founded on adultery — to maintenance proceedings. Practitioners in Uttar Pradesh should watch for rules that may follow; the logic will be persuasive elsewhere, including in Delhi.</p>

<h2>The transition to the BNSS regime</h2>

<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><h3>CrPC (until 30 June 2024)</h3><p>Section 125: maintenance of wife, children and parents. Section 125(4): no maintenance to a wife living in adultery, refusing without sufficient reason to live with the husband, or living separately by mutual consent.</p></div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><h3>BNSS (from 1 July 2024)</h3><p>Section 144: the same scheme, with Section 144(4) carrying the identical disqualifications. Electronic records are now proved under Section 63 of the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023, which preserves the certificate requirement.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>What this means in practice</h2>

<p>For husbands, the decision is a caution against treating Section 125(4) as a shortcut. The defence requires pleading and proving a continuing adulterous relationship through admissible evidence: authenticated electronic records with the statutory certificate, witnesses who can withstand cross-examination, and — where a specific person is named — an application seeking notice to him so that any finding can lawfully be recorded. An adultery plea that fails, apart from not defeating maintenance, can colour the court's view of the husband's bona fides.</p>

<p>For wives and claimants, the judgment reinforces that maintenance cannot be stalled by scandalous but unproved allegations. The Family Court's relaxed evidentiary gate under Section 14 works both ways: material comes in easily, but it earns no weight until proved. A claimant facing such a defence should insist on strict proof, press the absence of the certificate for every electronic record, and point out that a stray allegation is not "living in adultery" within the meaning of the provision.</p>

<div class="note"><p>Maintenance orders under Section 125 CrPC / Section 144 BNSS are quasi-civil in nature and turn on their own facts. This summary of a recent Allahabad High Court judgment is for general information only and is not legal advice.</p></div>
